# FFmpeg corresponding source and replacement instructions

Edit Atlas @EDIT_ATLAS_VERSION@ dynamically links to FFmpeg
@EDIT_ATLAS_FFMPEG_VERSION@ under the GNU Lesser General Public License
version 2.1 or later.

The complete corresponding FFmpeg source used for this release is available
from the Edit Atlas release page:

https://github.com/gabrielslrodrigues/edit-atlas/releases/download/v@EDIT_ATLAS_VERSION@/edit-atlas-@EDIT_ATLAS_VERSION@-ffmpeg-source.tar.xz

Verify the archive against `SHA256SUMS` from the same release. The archive
contains the original FFmpeg source archive, its SHA-512 digest, the exact
vcpkg FFmpeg port and patches, the pinned vcpkg commit, the Edit Atlas manifest
and triplets, and the build configuration relevant to FFmpeg.

## Rebuild FFmpeg

1. Check out Edit Atlas tag `v@EDIT_ATLAS_VERSION@` with its vcpkg submodule.
2. Confirm that vcpkg is at commit `@EDIT_ATLAS_VCPKG_BASELINE@`.
3. Copy the provided FFmpeg source archive into `vcpkg/downloads` if desired;
   vcpkg may otherwise download the same archive itself.
4. Bootstrap vcpkg and configure the appropriate release preset documented in
   the Edit Atlas README. vcpkg verifies the source digest and applies the
   included port patches before building FFmpeg.
5. To modify FFmpeg, use the included source and port as an overlay port, apply
   your changes, and rebuild Edit Atlas against that FFmpeg build.

The complete Edit Atlas application source is available from the same release
tag under the Apache License 2.0, so the application can also be rebuilt
against interface-compatible modified FFmpeg libraries.

## Replace the bundled FFmpeg libraries

Edit Atlas uses ordinary dynamically loaded FFmpeg libraries. Replace the
complete compatible libavcodec, libavformat, libavutil, and libswscale set
rather than mixing incompatible builds. Their package locations are:

- Windows: beside `bin/edit-atlas.exe`;
- Linux: in the private `lib/edit-atlas` or `lib64/edit-atlas` directory; and
- macOS: in `edit-atlas.app/Contents/Frameworks`.

Preserve the distributed filenames, symbolic links, and, on macOS, universal
x86_64 and ARM64 slices and install names. Current macOS packages use only an
ad-hoc signature. After replacing a library, reapply it with
`codesign --force --deep --sign - edit-atlas.app`. No signing identity, DRM, or
access control prevents replacement.
